From: sasha1@netcom.com (Alexander Chislenko)
Date: Mon, 3 Oct 1994 18:33:08 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-41] "Proof" in math

     Mathematical proofs may actually be checked - sometimes.
They are never really proofs in the formal sense though.
Nobody would waste time and paper to reference all supporting
statements refered to by the current theorem - down to the properties
of addition.
   A 'proof' is really a persuasion, a line of stepping stones:
" A apparently is less than B, which implies C, from which D is evident".
 
The desire to test these stepping stones, and the agreement with their
validity are usually based on the *intuition* of the researcher.
Which is formed by the professional culture, prevailing concepts
in the field, etc.
 
   Mathematics may be as informal as anything else - if not more so,
since your intuition in real-world disciplines is at least formed on
the basis of observation of non-contradictory phenomena (unless you
have a misfortune of living in a buggy simulation) - while in math
the subjects of your studies may be later proven non-existent.
 
 
  Also - I would be interested to see that proof of the sphere turning
inside out (that's a homeomorphism in 3-D, right?  I'd think that one
can do it only in 4-D...)
 
