Anyone attempting to understand a branch of mathemetics
from a theory point of view does study all the proofs relevant
to his field of study.

A review of the proofs may reveal how proofs may be generalized.
It shows how a proof may be reused in analogous situation. An
understanding of why theorems are true increases one's mathematical
intutition. So even if old proofs of theorems are not reviewed by
professors, they will be reviewed by graduate students trying
to learn the field.


An exception may be huge combinational proofs like the 4 color
theorem which may be checked by computer.

A graduate student finding a flaw in an existing proof can
expect praise at least.

