From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Tue, 4 Oct 94 3:38:13 WET DST
Subject: [#94-10-45] "Proof" in math

Paul Elliott writes:
> 
> Anyone attempting to understand a branch of mathemetics
> from a theory point of view does study all the proofs relevant
> to his field of study.

   True, but one often learns so much that one forgets the details
behind all the theorems and math leading up to a result and merely
takes the result is true (having understood it once) This applies
especially to long proofs of big results. One forgets the "details"
but remembers the essense. Given time, one could rederive
the results (for instance, I have forgotten the details of the
proof of the Fundamental Theorem of Abelian groups, but I could
probably rederive it in an hour or two, especially if I glanced
at a textbook. However, I do remember the theorem, and that it was
proven by the instructor) That's what I meant by my statement.
After a while, the theorems become part of the mathematician's
intuition, although he may have long forgotten at how the result
was arrived at. Thus, a mathematician analyzing a proof will often
simply say "the conditions are correct, the theorem can be applied,
this step is correct, now to check the rest" without reflecting
on the entire meaning of the theorem. This ability to narrow
one's thinking to a specific area while ignoring other information
is, I contend, one of our great strengths. Computers often end up
analyzing information which is irrelevent to the overall picture
(consider how humans play chess vs computers)



   
  

