Yes, this is a rather gentle method, and the method I plan to use myself. 
I just don't like dramatic, irreversible events. On the other hand, this
method is not easily reversible. As you rely more and more on external
systems, you will become very handicapped if they are suddenly removed (by
accident, lack of funds or other reasons). Just read the description in
Marooned in Realtime by Vinge of what happened to the close-to-singularity
human when he was suddenly cut off from his system; he was in coma for a
long time until he managed to get his internal mental systems back online. 


> This has the advantage that most of the hardware needed to attempt it
>probably exists today.

There is currently no good neural-electronic links, although we are
getting closer and closer. But a direct connection is not absolutely
necessary from the beginning, we can use non-neural interfaces to increase
our potential today. We can already use our computers and networks for
communications, information processing and retrieval. What is needed is
just better and better interfaces, moving us and the system closer
together. 

I already regard my glasses, my calculator, my pager, my computer account
and my Web pages as a kind of extensions of myself, not yet parts of me,
but not that far from it. They provide new functions or enchancements, and
although they are partially replaceable they form a kind of "exo-self"
[1]. By the way, we better make the neural connections easy to change when
we build them. Imagine being stuck with a RS232 port in your skull when
everybody else is using SCSI. :-)


> The problems are that there is no way to know without a lot of work
>whether it will succeed in transferring enough of my identity to
>satisfy me, and that the human nervous system may not be adaptable
>enough to handle a high bandwidth connection. Nerves in the adult brain
>don't normally grow enough (although progress is being made on overcoming
>this - p. 31 of the Oct. 1994 Scientific American reports that eye
>transplants may be possible before too long). Peripheral nerves may
>not be able to handle the bandwidth, and their less direct connection
>to the important parts of the brain may make retraining harder.

I think the main bottleneck is the mental software. We must learn to cope
with massive amounts of information, rapid changes and such things. 
Increased neural connectivity, nootropic drugs and such can help, but we
need to find ways to enchance the mental software to become truly
effective. One way is to create filters, agents, expert systems and other
systems and incorporate them into out exoselves, doing as much work as
possible (essentially the main idea of this method of uploading). In the
end the exoself will run the neuronal brain, not the other way around. 

Another question is how much identity we want to preserve. One thing I
dislike about normal uploading scenarios is that you get a lot of extra
stuff you might not need or want, and removing it could prove very hard. 
Do we really want to have *all* of our identities, or is it better to just
have the good parts (with maybe the other parts archived for future needs
or reference)? 


[1]: "Exoself" is a term borrowed from Greg Egan's excellent "Permutation
City", originally denoting the systems surrounding an uploaded
personality, providing information, virtual reality and modifications. I
use the term rather loosely to denote systems connected to the self in a
cooperative way, working as an extended mind/body. 

