From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Tue, 4 Oct 1994 05:43:34 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-10-50] Neural Darwinism

Lucianna Haill: 
> Anyone with any views on Neural Darwinism (that the brain selects 
> advantageous neural connections through evolutonary development)
> ...cerebral improvement at  neuronal level.

Parallels have long been drawn between evolution and learning,
under the more general rubric "adaptation".  The recent
success of genetic algorithms (essentially a formalized,
computerized form of Darwinian selection) as learning algorithms 
has put those parallels in sharper focus.  Genetic algorithms are 
among the most general purpose of learning algorithms, in that
they can be applied to many kinds of nonlinear and combinatoric
structures where other learning algorithms fail.  Genetic
algorithms are also "embarassingly parallel": their performance is 
directly proportional to the amount of parallization.  Thus, some
form of parallel Darwinian selection might be quite suitable for 
learning in the brain.

Indeed, early childhood learning exhibits one of the symptoms of
Darwinism: babies start out with a huge surplus of neurons, most
of which are killed off in the process of early learning skills
of locomotion, language, pattern recognition, et. al. by age 
five.  In terms of computational difficulty, the learning performance 
of 1-5 year old children dwarfs that of any other evolutures
on the planet, including human adults.  Neural death seems
to greatly improve the learning process, as longs as there
are expendable neurons around.

Neuron death plays no role after early childhood; any adult 
Darwinian learning would have to use some mechanism
besides killing of unfit neurons.  In computer learning, it's often
the case that specific tasks can be learned by customized
algorithms faster than they can by general purpose genetic
algorithms.  This is probably also true for some brain tasks:
specific tasks can be performed by specific parallel processes
better than they can by the general purpose and rather wasteful 
Darwinian process.  On the other hand, learning on the massive
scale humans engage in is a recent evolutionary development, which
means it can't be much more complicated than what goes on
in other mammals.  Those aspects unique to humans might be 
dominated by a process like neural Darwisism that is general
purpose and can be simply encoded in the genes.  It's going
to be complicated by skills inherited from our mammalian
ancestors (locomotion, pattern recognition, etc.) 
and other kinds of learning (eg analogy, or the comparison of
independently learned concepts for shared structure).

Last year I critiqued for William Calvin a chapter from
a book he was writing on neural darwinism.  Perhaps it's hit
the stands by now, or will within the next year.

